WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:01.670 align:start position:0%
 
i have been playing an early build of  baldur's gate 3 for over  15 hours now which may have been enough  time to see most of the early access  content if i weren't switching back and  forth between runs  or creating new characters so often  after being so excited to play bg3  and being drip fed content over the past  year or so i was certainly full of  anticipation to jump into the world and  while i had high hopes that the  storytelling and systemic gameplay was  going to be interesting to play with  and attempt to break i didn't expect  baldur's gate 3 to address one of my  deepest pet peeves in rpgs  and that's how the genre handles failure  in boulder's gate 3  failure actually becomes fun let me  start by saying even in the face of the  early access bugs and glitches the game  both plays and looks great  and as someone who's gone through a  bunch of real-life dnd campaigns seeing  a lot of those table top elements come  to life  really is incredible but i'm here to  talk about narrative  and story today now there are a few ways  that some of my favorite role-playing  games have handled bumps in the road or  failures or end states in their quests  take fallout for example when you are  teased with a prospect of a dialogue  choice in the conversation box but you  don't have the relevant stat  so you're left thinking what if i were  able to select the option but in many  cases never getting the chance  to basically failing at the first hurdle  other games address any failure to  adhere to the quest line with an  unsatisfactory full stop  basically cutting that story thread  there and then taking that as a yep  you failed that's the end of that but  you know better luck next time  but baldur's gate 3 immediately felt  like a different beast with how it  handles its narrative  story beats and the options that are  available to the player  in my first hours i tried to resist the  urge to save and reload if i failed  dialogue options or dice rolls and i  honestly  recommend you resist that same desire  despite how tempting it may be  in fact doing so may mean you'll miss  out on much of the ebb and flow  of some of the dialogues and smaller  stories within the early access build  failure and booked up dice rolls are  just as important to the story and  development of your character  and overall experience as successes and  natural 20s are  while i know it's fun to see the word  success pop up on your screen as you  roll well  i've come to realize that in boulder's  gate 3 at least a video game where i  only succeed  isn't what i want and it really isn't  representative of the tabletop game that  it's built upon  in this way bg3 really is an extension  of the dungeons and dragons  tabletop storytelling because no matter  your build your stats  all the choices you make the chances of  failing are  never zero and your success is never  guaranteed  in that way it's up to the players to  figure out creative ways around problems  and navigate hiccups in your quest  progression  and this is something that baldur's gate  3 seems to excel at  even in its early access not once in my  15 hours of playing have i encountered a  quote  fail state that i wasn't able to work my  way around  be that through some clever exploration  dialogue choices  approaching with different characters or  in one or two cases  violence  the systemic storytelling and gameplay  of baldur's gate 3 means that the game  almost  scabs over any damage you may deal to it  or any cuts you make into its narrative  threads  one small but interesting example of  this is when i encountered lyzelle  trapped in a cage on my second run  on the next meeting the tieflings  guarding her had been in combat with the  bandits that i'd convinced to leave the  ruins that i wanted to explore  but the male tiefling died in a battle  that happened completely without my  knowing  in this case i noticed that the dialogue  had been written unvoiced from multiple  perspectives  just in case an issue like this may  occur during the first meeting the  conversation would involve both  tieflings while in this instance the  game just kept on trucking with the one  remaining taking the lead  that allowed me to progress seemingly  without any major hitches  in a way that felt so organic that i  would have had no idea  that anything was different if i hadn't  already visited that section once before  now if i took on those quests and  actions in a different order perhaps  freeing lyzelle  before i spoke to the bandits things  would have played out in a new direction  as well  again this was a pretty minor example of  the system in action but that doesn't  make it any less interesting  especially as the rest of the game is  built in the same way and i couldn't  help but go back to thinking about  previous rpgs and how  this quest or dialogue may have broken  if this were any other title  this also speaks to the replayability  larian is aiming for with both the early  access version of baldur's gate 3 as  well as the final game  with that in mind i want to talk about  the absolutely daft number of options  that were available to you out in the  world  and when interacting with npcs that  inhabit it there are unique dialogue  options available depending on your race  class background and whether you're  tapping into your illified powers  as well as the combination of all those  working in tandem and that's before we  even get into the possibility of  communicating with the dead  or the wildlife  how about this you can stay if you keep  those ugly feet on the ground  these trees are mine follow the rules  and i won't have to intervene  got it even something as seemingly  simple as a locked door can be taken on  in a staggering number of ways  from lock picking stealing keys using  eldritch powers to smash the thing down  teleporting through a hole in the wall  or jumping in via the roof i've honestly  yet to be disappointed when it comes to  thinking  hmm i wonder if this will work there are  also other ways that a supposed fail  state can be conquered  or even the potentiality of one can be  minimized you can initiate contacts and  conversations or interact with the world  with any of your characters  not just your created one which in turn  allows you to tap  into the more beneficial racial traits  and stats if you fail a check on an  inanimate object  you can switch to a new character and  thus potentially create another  opportunity to try again  if you don't like the way a conversation  is going you can still manipulate the  rest of your party outside of that chat  and put them into some more advantageous  positions or manipulate the world as the  conversation continues  with this element in particular seeming  to carry over many inspirations from  divinity original sin  now knowing that a borked dice roll  isn't going to change things and that  even a high chance of success  isn't a guaranteed win also made me much  braver and more open to the ideas of  taking risks  and trying new options while exploring  bg3 in previous rpgs i would usually  pick any option or event that would  grant me the highest chance of success  or grant me the opportunity to see as  much of the content as i could  but knowing that it wasn't quite as  black and white as that in baldur's gate  3  options that were riskier narratively  interesting or that i thought  suited my character became much more  alluring and exciting  again that feels like something very d  tapping into the story and playing on  the background you created yourself  all while the game drip feeds you  opportunity after opportunity  to continue stories dialogue and  narratives  both on the macro and micro level  reaffirming the player that there are so  many options here  before you delve into becoming a murder  hobo  although if you want to play that way  you do you  you made a potential threat into a  certain one  we don't need to pick every fight the  approach boulders gate 3 takes to  success and failure really  is quite a refreshing one and something  i am really excited to see built upon  when you factor in its depth at this  early stage in its production  and add on the scope that larian wants  the game to be i really can't see a  situation  where your options are going to be found  lacking they're only going to become  more numerous  as time goes on and it's no surprise  that it's recommended  you run through the early access version  more than once as you would be hard  pressed to have the exact same  experience  every time i want you to go into  baldur's gate 3 not thinking of failure  as failing instead it's more of a hidden  option  in a dialogue tree that will allow you  to progress your experience even in the  face  of the number one on your dice it just  might not progress in the way you expect  it to  or in the way you want it to and after  all a hero or adventure that has nothing  but winds and victories in it  doesn't make for the most exciting tale  to tell down the tavern  my friend every story benefits from a  dragon  until we meet again that's been my main  takeaway from bowlers gate 3 so far in  the  just shy of 20 hours that i've got to  play it but i'd love to know what you're  excited for when it comes to traveling  across faerun and the sword coast there  is a lot to tap into in bg3 so we've got  plenty more content planned  so to be sure you don't miss out on that  make sure you hit the subscribe button  down below and let your thoughts be  known down in the comment section my  name is dave you can find me and all my  rpg ramblings over on twitter at a  regular dave and until next  time thank you so much for watching  you

